1
00:00:06,199 --> 00:00:08,570
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:08,571 --> 00:00:13,130
This time, let's learn about expression methods according to material.

3
00:00:13,210 --> 00:00:16,851
You can think of it as an in-depth version of Chapter 3.

4
00:00:17,710 --> 00:00:22,270
There are various elements that make a material look different.

5
00:00:22,271 --> 00:00:24,190
Let me divide them roughly into four.

6
00:00:25,765 --> 00:00:36,480
Number one, the roughness of the surface. Number two, reflectance and transmittance. Number three, inherent color. Number four, the inherent form that appears due to the material.

7
00:00:38,365 --> 00:00:40,570
Shall we go through each one?

8
00:00:40,571 --> 00:00:48,211
Number one, the roughness of the surface. In Chapter 3 we briefly learned about material characteristics according to light.

9
00:00:49,025 --> 00:00:53,450
We briefly learned that the reflection of light changes depending on the roughness of the surface.

10
00:00:54,960 --> 00:01:04,601
To say it again, if the roughness is close to zero, the light reflects very honestly.

11
00:01:09,339 --> 00:01:16,311
Reflecting honestly means the image clearly forms, like smooth metal or a mirror.

12
00:01:17,749 --> 00:01:24,920
Conversely, if the roughness is high, the light can't align and scatters.

13
00:01:26,784 --> 00:01:31,260
Like the surface of paper or leather.

14
00:01:31,261 --> 00:01:38,780
Varying the surface roughness lets you express various materials when painting.

15
00:01:38,781 --> 00:01:41,500
Even the same metal, depending on its polish.

16
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:51,001
It can sparkle like a mirror, or it can blur softly like this.

17
00:01:52,914 --> 00:01:57,541
And depending on the crafting detail, it can look different too.

18
00:01:59,189 --> 00:02:03,800
Let's talk about number two, reflectance and transmittance.

19
00:02:03,801 --> 00:02:07,361
The higher an object's reflectance, the more light it reflects.

20
00:02:15,559 --> 00:02:16,559
It'd be like this, right?

21
00:02:19,049 --> 00:02:20,049
That's obvious.

22
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:28,360
For reflection, there's specular reflection and diffuse reflection.

23
00:02:32,565 --> 00:02:38,590
The element by which we perceive an object's color is diffuse reflection off the surface.

24
00:02:42,915 --> 00:02:48,270
If this diffuse reflectance becomes infinitely high, the object looks white.

25
00:02:50,109 --> 00:02:54,880
Because if it reflects all the colors of visible light, it appears white.

26
00:02:56,595 --> 00:02:58,220
You know the three primary colors of light, right?

27
00:02:59,904 --> 00:03:04,140
When all colors of light mix, it looks white.

28
00:03:04,141 --> 00:03:13,550
Conversely, if the reflectance becomes infinitely low, it can't reflect any light, so the object would look black.

29
00:03:13,551 --> 00:03:16,870
Since there's no light coming to the eyes, it's just dark.

30
00:03:18,225 --> 00:03:23,690
However, a material with perfectly zero reflectance doesn't exist, at least on Earth.

31
00:03:23,691 --> 00:03:29,530
Because of that, even when depicting a black object, you can add value shading.

32
00:03:38,114 --> 00:03:39,114
Like this.

33
00:03:42,035 --> 00:03:49,950
And black materials hardly undergo diffuse reflection, but depending on polish, specular reflection can occur.

34
00:03:49,951 --> 00:03:59,550
For example, a material like black shoes can show strong highlights like this, depending on the polish and how much it's coated.

35
00:04:02,359 --> 00:04:12,010
In this way, white and black follow reflectance, but they also need different rendering depending on the material composition.

36
00:04:14,070 --> 00:04:18,760
For example, a mirror has over ninety percent reflectance.

37
00:04:18,761 --> 00:04:20,519
But why doesn't it look white?

38
00:04:21,750 --> 00:04:25,880
It's related to number one, the roughness of the surface.

39
00:04:25,885 --> 00:04:31,480
An object that feels white always has a rough surface.

40
00:04:38,015 --> 00:04:42,130
You can take white paper or white leather as examples.

41
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:49,130
Only when light scatters and diffuse reflection occurs does it finally look white.

42
00:04:50,620 --> 00:04:56,990
A mirror surface isn't rough, so it doesn't scatter the light, with respect to the incident ray.

43
00:05:02,940 --> 00:05:06,030
It reflects the light symmetrically.

44
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:16,480
We call this specular reflection.

45
00:05:16,481 --> 00:05:24,841
Because of that, it doesn't look white, but lets the incident light be seen by our eyes as is.

46
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:33,050
However, a mirror is actually behind transparent glass.

47
00:05:35,350 --> 00:05:39,880
A metal material is coated on it.

48
00:05:39,881 --> 00:05:42,120
Glass can't reflect light.

49
00:05:44,010 --> 00:05:48,250
It transmits it, and this is transmittance.

50
00:05:53,505 --> 00:05:58,261
Usually glass has about ninety percent transmittance.

51
00:06:03,535 --> 00:06:09,720
A mirror's reflectance is taken care of by the metal material coated behind the glass.

52
00:06:11,610 --> 00:06:18,020
The reason I'm explaining this transmittance is because the skin we draw a lot has high transmittance.

53
00:06:18,650 --> 00:06:27,900
You should know that the color we know as skin is actually the collection of the muscles, ligaments, blood vessels, and fat wrapped by the skin.

54
00:06:29,175 --> 00:06:34,000
Many illustrators work hard to draw transparent skin.

55
00:06:34,001 --> 00:06:37,401
Especially, they carefully consider subsurface scattering.

56
00:06:41,075 --> 00:06:49,420
We also learned that even a transparent object like this reflects light if the angle of incidence exceeds a certain angle.

57
00:06:49,421 --> 00:06:50,620
That's called total internal reflection.

58
00:06:53,685 --> 00:06:58,630
Don't misunderstand; it's a different concept from specular reflection.

59
00:07:02,655 --> 00:07:12,250
The angle at which total internal reflection occurs also differs depending on the material, but considering that too makes my head hurt too much, so just think of it moderately.

60
00:07:14,430 --> 00:07:21,760
Depending on this transmittance, when drawing glass, you can distinguish between glass with strong transmittance and glass with weak transmittance.

61
00:07:23,990 --> 00:07:28,081
Colored glass naturally has lower transmittance, of course.

62
00:07:29,420 --> 00:07:37,500
And glass with a roughened surface also has lower transmittance and undergoes diffuse reflection, so it looks blurry and white.

63
00:07:39,675 --> 00:07:43,960
Steamy glass works on a similar principle too.

64
00:07:43,961 --> 00:07:51,320
Anyway, if you draw while thinking about this reflectance and transmittance, material can become more varied.

65
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:52,680
Considering this point.

66
00:07:55,055 --> 00:07:59,960
Even if you cover it all with similar colors, you can paint it so it looks like different materials.

67
00:08:12,095 --> 00:08:16,451
So that, though similar in color, they can look like different materials.

68
00:08:25,470 --> 00:08:26,520
You can paint it.

69
00:08:26,521 --> 00:08:28,520
Let me paint an object.

70
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:33,289
What is inherent color, color?

71
00:08:33,290 --> 00:08:39,450
It's related to that electromagnetic wave range we commonly call visible light.

72
00:08:39,451 --> 00:08:42,409
Think of the rainbow colors; it's simple.

73
00:08:42,410 --> 00:08:47,290
An object absorbs part of this electromagnetic wave range and reflects part of it.

74
00:08:48,830 --> 00:08:58,601
In this way, a red object only reflects the red visible light range, so it looks red, and blue reflects only the blue range, so it's blue.

75
00:08:59,670 --> 00:09:03,640
Also, an object's inherent color is closely related to inherent value.

76
00:09:04,855 --> 00:09:13,760
I said metal has high reflectance. When it's pure white metal, that is, like silver.

77
00:09:14,005 --> 00:09:23,961
It has the highest reflectance, and colored metal, like copper or gold, has somewhat lower reflectance.

78
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:34,000
For the inherent value of colors, this yellow is the highest.

79
00:09:35,230 --> 00:09:38,240
And blue is the lowest.

80
00:09:38,494 --> 00:09:43,120
In this way, painting the brightest yellow and the brightest blue.

81
00:09:44,630 --> 00:09:47,640
And converting to grayscale reveals it.

82
00:09:48,805 --> 00:09:49,840
There's a difference in value.

83
00:09:51,260 --> 00:09:58,281
The reason this inherent value according to color is important is because the material can look different depending on the object's value.

84
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:07,390
For example, even when drawing a picture with various metals mixed together, it's important to understand the material and color characteristics and draw.

85
00:10:08,595 --> 00:10:14,620
If the polish is similar, then naturally a silver ornament will be brighter than a copper ornament.

86
00:10:19,740 --> 00:10:24,270
You should be able to grasp and draw this well.

87
00:10:24,271 --> 00:10:26,990
Number four, the inherent form that appears due to the material.

88
00:10:28,280 --> 00:10:31,690
Every material has a form we imagine.

89
00:10:31,691 --> 00:10:36,250
If you're told to draw a water droplet, you'll naturally draw it smooth and round.

90
00:10:37,750 --> 00:10:41,640
If you draw a metal weapon, you'll draw it very sharp and pointed.

91
00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:46,120
Imagine a knife made of charcoal.

92
00:10:46,121 --> 00:10:49,761
It can't be sharp; it's blunt and bumpy.

93
00:10:51,130 --> 00:10:57,260
Also think about the difference in form between a ball made of metal and a ball made of leather.

94
00:10:57,261 --> 00:11:01,660
A metal ball is stamped with a press, so it has no visible seams.

95
00:11:01,661 --> 00:11:06,780
But a leather ball is made by joining several pieces, so the seams are visible.

96
00:11:08,170 --> 00:11:14,371
That's the reason I said in Chapter 5's explanation that when drawing clothing, you should draw the seam lines well.

97
00:11:15,880 --> 00:11:22,440
In this way, even with reflectance and color where the material is hard to tell apart, just through this kind of form rendering.

98
00:11:23,855 --> 00:11:26,760
It lets you identify the material.

99
00:11:26,761 --> 00:11:32,920
Giving it a poked-dotted form makes it look like a soft, thick material.

100
00:11:34,590 --> 00:11:42,800
It's important to render the surface material well, but it's also important to grasp the material characteristics and draw the form.

101
00:11:44,280 --> 00:11:47,690
I gave a boring theoretical talk.

102
00:11:47,691 --> 00:11:54,890
The theoretical talk about material is important, but I think most of you want to see the painting skill.

103
00:11:54,891 --> 00:11:56,410
So I'll draw directly.

104
00:12:01,950 --> 00:12:05,920
Let me draw with materials commonly used in pictures.

105
00:12:05,921 --> 00:12:10,000
First, metal. In that chapter I drew smooth metal.

106
00:12:10,080 --> 00:12:12,560
Let me draw without much explanation.

107
00:12:57,375 --> 00:13:01,651
Next, let me express bumpy, rough metal.

108
00:13:04,805 --> 00:13:08,361
Using a brush to draw a rough surface like this.

109
00:13:28,675 --> 00:13:32,951
And what makes this process easy is custom brushes.

110
00:13:33,630 --> 00:13:35,150
Good for cutting down time, right?

111
00:13:49,079 --> 00:13:50,080
I'm drawing with a custom brush.

112
00:13:50,081 --> 00:13:52,080
Now let's draw leather.

113
00:13:56,945 --> 00:14:00,451
It has lower reflectance than metal, so the contrast isn't strong.

114
00:14:01,695 --> 00:14:06,170
There are many kinds of leather, but I mostly draw moderately coated leather.

115
00:14:07,365 --> 00:14:10,480
Untreated leather doesn't have much shine.

116
00:14:10,481 --> 00:14:12,800
That's the result of polishing or coating.

117
00:14:21,475 --> 00:14:28,871
Following the inherent form of the material, expressing the leather's seam lines and texture is great.

118
00:14:33,935 --> 00:14:39,480
Leather that's heavily polished and coated, like shoes, is drawn with a shine.

119
00:14:49,130 --> 00:14:53,720
Depending on the polish of the leather, the highlight can get even stronger.

120
00:15:10,859 --> 00:15:16,010
For cloth, highlights barely form, and it's not hard.

121
00:15:16,011 --> 00:15:19,571
However, you need to draw considering the folds well.

122
00:15:21,135 --> 00:15:25,800
For folds, grasp the meeting points and the direction of gravity, then draw.

123
00:15:27,160 --> 00:15:31,319
Natural folds add to the realistic appeal of a drawing.

124
00:15:32,540 --> 00:15:37,230
Approaching it theoretically is good, but mastering it through copy-study is better.

125
00:15:44,035 --> 00:15:48,510
Following the shadow method we learned, let's also add shadows.

126
00:16:38,695 --> 00:16:41,520
Because there's no color, it looks boring.

127
00:16:41,521 --> 00:16:42,521
So.

128
00:16:43,290 --> 00:16:48,181
Let me change the light to warm and the darkness to cool.

129
00:17:16,839 --> 00:17:17,839
It's the light color I often use.

130
00:17:17,840 --> 00:17:19,440
Let me also add a bit of color to the background.

131
00:17:24,944 --> 00:17:28,691
Let me change this cloth I just drew into various materials.

132
00:17:30,489 --> 00:17:33,260
First, let me change it to a transparent material.

133
00:17:33,261 --> 00:17:40,780
When expressing a transparent material like this, I tend to use the layer property, lower the opacity, and retouch.

134
00:17:42,249 --> 00:17:46,650
This way, you can easily create that transparent feel.

135
00:17:46,651 --> 00:17:53,891
Also, for highly transparent materials like vinyl, most light transmits, and only the highlight shows.

136
00:17:55,069 --> 00:17:57,280
So you just need to draw the highlight.

137
00:17:57,439 --> 00:18:08,560
An easy way is to render it roughly in the shape of vinyl, then raise the contrast and use the screen layer property among layer properties.

138
00:18:13,209 --> 00:18:15,820
Since it's a vinyl material, it can't be rough, of course.

139
00:18:16,939 --> 00:18:18,620
I'm smoothing it out.

140
00:18:23,979 --> 00:18:28,810
Then change it to the screen property.

141
00:18:28,811 --> 00:18:35,130
Since vinyl also isn't a hundred percent transmittance, I laid a bit of white underneath.

142
00:18:40,034 --> 00:18:41,034
Not hard, right?

143
00:18:44,594 --> 00:18:48,110
And should I call it a hologram material?

144
00:18:48,111 --> 00:18:52,350
When expressing this kind of rainbow material, you can also use Photoshop features.

145
00:18:54,434 --> 00:18:59,310
Let me create it using the curve tool.

146
00:18:59,311 --> 00:19:01,310
I said I'd create it.

147
00:19:01,390 --> 00:19:09,470
This method of using Photoshop features is not something I recommend to aspiring artists who are actively studying drawing.

148
00:19:11,580 --> 00:19:16,430
Only professionals making money from drawing should watch it secretly.

149
00:19:16,431 --> 00:19:18,910
I adjust the graph of the curve tool here and there.

150
00:19:18,994 --> 00:19:20,430
Until I get the feel I want.

151
00:19:23,684 --> 00:19:25,200
It came out interestingly.

152
00:19:25,201 --> 00:19:28,400
Nice.

153
00:19:28,401 --> 00:19:32,880
If you use the curve tool well, you can easily express various materials.

154
00:19:34,380 --> 00:19:42,380
Aspiring artists, press the back button quickly.

155
00:19:42,429 --> 00:19:49,980
Using the method of making transparent and hologram materials, I'll actually apply it to the picture we drew in Chapter 5.

156
00:19:59,804 --> 00:20:06,600
Using Photoshop features like this, you can so easily create various materials like hologram.

157
00:20:08,509 --> 00:20:12,441
However, for harmony with the picture, retouching is absolutely necessary.

158
00:20:14,389 --> 00:20:18,840
I was worried because it's a design that could be polarizing.

159
00:20:18,841 --> 00:20:24,840
Still, having drawn a picture of a material I hadn't drawn before, it's been great learning for me too.

160
00:20:24,841 --> 00:20:28,001
I hope it helps you too.

161
00:20:30,174 --> 00:20:35,450
Using curve adjustment for material rendering like this, you shouldn't overuse it.

162
00:20:36,830 --> 00:20:42,080
You end up relying on this method, and in the end, you can't render without it.

163
00:20:43,610 --> 00:20:47,660
Use it when you can draw it yourself.

164
00:20:47,661 --> 00:20:51,301
Also, if you use it too much, the picture loses its center.

165
00:20:52,469 --> 00:21:01,480
Through moderate use and retouching, I should be able to continue in the direction I set my navigation and was heading.

166
00:21:03,899 --> 00:21:09,470
This time, I explained various materials and how to draw them.

167
00:21:10,859 --> 00:21:15,820
Next time, let's discuss detail rendering and finishing the picture.
